--- !u!114 &580902050952586443
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: '<b>Skybox Material:</b> <i>GS-Procedural-Sky</i> (shader <i>Gas-Station/Procedural Night Sky</i>). It starts from three colours blended by height (top, middle, bottom) in a very dark teal, and adds what the shader computes itself: clouds drifting slowly, lit from below by a warm glow along the horizon (the town), stars that the clouds cover, and a moon with a soft glow. All its colours are typed as small linear numbers, because the sky must be about a hundred times darker than the lights. Try <i>Cover</i>, <i>Drift</i> and the moon''s <i>Direction</i>; in the fog, the moon and the horizon glow read most. The simpler <i>GS-Gradient-Sky</i> (the three colours only) is in the same folder.'
--- !u!114 &1395869729966467409
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
